# Who Actually Uses Bigsfield — A Creator Type Study
### The Same Platform, Eight Completely Different Products

> **The honest truth about Bigsfield:** It doesn't have one type of user. It has eight — and they barely overlap. A filmmaker and a DTC brand marketer both have Bigsfield accounts and both swear by it. They use almost none of the same products.
>
> This document maps Bigsfield's full product suite to the actual types of creators who use it — not a linear "journey" that forces everyone into the same path, but an honest look at who each product was built for, who it doesn't serve, and why a single platform can contain all of these people at once.

---

## Why Creator Type Matters More Than Journey Stage

Most platform analyses describe a journey: discover → learn → create → master. The problem is that journey assumes everyone starts at the same place and moves in the same direction.

They don't.

A professional video editor who's been cutting films for ten years doesn't arrive at Bigsfield as a beginner. They walk straight past the Viral Presets, ignore the Academy, and want to know one thing: does the Premiere Pro plugin work inside their existing timeline?

A brand manager at a DTC company doesn't care about Cinema Studio's camera controls. They need product shots and ad variations by Thursday.

A faceless content creator building a YouTube channel doesn't need lipsync or editorial motion graphics. They need automated video production that requires zero on-camera presence.

Same platform. Completely different products. Completely different definitions of value.

Understanding Bigsfield through creator types — not journey stages — is what lets you see how a platform this broad can exist without being unfocused. Each creator type finds their Bigsfield within the larger product. The platform's breadth is not a positioning problem; it's the product strategy itself.

